.\" Manpage for escpmd.
.\" Contact solutions@agilemindswork.uk to fix typos.
.TH ESCPMD 1 "May 2026" "escpmd 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
escpmd \- render Markdown to an Epson ESC/P 2 byte stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B escpmd
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ FILE .\|.\|.]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B escpmd
is a Unix-style filter that reads a Markdown document and writes the
equivalent ESC/P 2 byte stream to standard output (or to the file given
with
.BR -o ).
With no
.I FILE
arguments, the document is read from standard input.  Multiple files
are concatenated and separated by a form-feed.
.PP
The output is the same flavour of ESC/P emitted by
.BR escp (1)
\(em both binaries share the same code generator.  The result is meant
to be piped directly to a dot-matrix printer or capture file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
escpmd README.md | lp -d pp404
cat doc.md | escpmd --cpi 12 --quality lq > pp404.bin
.fi
.RE
.PP
Markdown is parsed with the bundled
.B md4c
library, configured for the GitHub Flavoured dialect (CommonMark plus
tables, strikethrough and permissive autolinks).
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Output
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-output \fIFILE\fR
Write the output bytes to \fIFILE\fR instead of standard output.
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-no\-init
Skip the prologue.  By default, escpmd emits
.B ESC @
followed by the body-style options (quality, cpi, font, lpi, margins)
at document start.  Use this flag when the surrounding pipeline already
emits an initialisation sequence (typically via
.BR escp (1)).
.TP
.B \-F, \-\-no\-ff
Skip the trailing form-feed and final
.BR "ESC @" .
.SS Body style
These options control the document's body text (everything that is not
a heading, code block, list bullet, etc.).
.TP
.B \-\-cpi \fIN\fR
Body pitch in characters per inch.  Accepts 10 (default), 12 or 15.
.TP
.B \-\-font \fINAME\fR
Body font family.  Same names as
.BR "escp \-\-font" :
.IR roman ,
.IR sans ,
.IR courier ,
.IR prestige ,
.IR script ,
.IR ocr-a ,
.IR ocr-b ,
.IR orator ,
.IR orator-c ,
.IR data-block ,
.IR data-large .
Default is
.IR roman .
.TP
.B \-\-quality \fIdraft|lq\fR
Body print quality.  Default is
.IR lq .
Note that fenced code blocks always switch to
.I draft
quality regardless of this setting.
.TP
.B \-\-lpi \fIN\fR
Lines per inch.  Default is 6.
.TP
.B \-\-left\-margin \fIN\fR, \-\-right\-margin \fIN\fR
Set printer left/right margin in character columns at the active pitch.
By default no margin is emitted.  When both are set, escpmd also uses
\fIright - left\fR as the printable width for software word\(hywrap and
the thematic\(hybreak rule (unless overridden by
.BR \-\-page\-width ).
The hardware margins are still written via
.BR "ESC l" / "ESC Q"
in the prologue as a safety net so the printer firmware will also
enforce them.
.TP
.B \-\-page\-width \fIN\fR
Explicit printable width in character columns at the body pitch.
Drives word\(hywrap of paragraphs and the width of the thematic\(hybreak
rule.  Overrides any width derived from
.BR \-\-left\-margin / \-\-right\-margin .
Default is unset (no software wrap; long lines flow to the printer
unbroken and are wrapped in hardware if a right margin was set).
.TP
.B \-\-charset \fINAME\fR
Output character encoding (anything
.BR iconv (3)
accepts: \fIISO-8859-1\fR, \fIISO-8859-15\fR, \fIASCII\fR, \fICP437\fR,
…). Default is \fIISO-8859-1\fR. UTF-8 input is converted to this
encoding with \fB//TRANSLIT\fR fallback; un-mappable codepoints become
\fB?\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-smileys
Expand GitHub-style emoji shortcodes (\fB:smile:\fR, \fB:wink:\fR,
\fB:heart:\fR, …) to ASCII equivalents. See
.IR docs/escpmd-mapping.md
for the full table. Unknown shortcodes are passed through verbatim.
.TP
.B \-\-link\-urls
After the underlined link text, also print \fB " (url)"\fR for
explicit references like \fB[text](url)\fR. By default escpmd prints
just the underlined text. Autolinks (\fB<https://…>\fR) always print as
the underlined URL.
.SS Information
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.SH MARKDOWN MAPPING
.B escpmd
translates each Markdown construct to a fixed ESC/P sequence.  The
complete table lives in
.IR docs/escpmd-mapping.md ;
the highlights are:
.TP
\fBHeadings\fR
H1 = double-width + double-height + bold.
H2 = double-width + bold.
H3 = bold + underline.
H4\(enH6 = bold.
.TP
\fBInline emphasis\fR
\fB**strong**\fR \(rA bold (\fBESC E\fR / \fBESC F\fR).
\fB*emphasis*\fR \(rA italic (\fBESC 4\fR / \fBESC 5\fR).
\fB~~strike~~\fR \(rA underline (ESC/P has no strikethrough).
.TP
\fBCode\fR
Inline \fB`code`\fR \(rA Courier + condensed.
Fenced code blocks \(rA Courier + condensed + draft quality.
.TP
\fBLists\fR
\fB- item\fR \(rA `*\~item' at the appropriate indent.
\fB1. item\fR \(rA `1.\~item' with a counter per nesting level.
.TP
\fBBlock quote\fR
Body is italicised.
.TP
\fBThematic break\fR (\fB---\fR)
Emits a line of 72 hyphens followed by LF.
.TP
\fBLinks\fR
The visible text is underlined.  Autolinks (\fB<https://x>\fR) print
the underlined URL.  Pass
.B \-\-link\-urls
to also write \fB\~(url)\fR after the underlined text for explicit
references.
.TP
\fBImages\fR
Alt text is printed in italic.
.TP
\fBTables\fR
Cells are separated by \fB\~|\~\fR; header cells are bold.
.TP
\fBUTF\(hy8\fR
Input is decoded by md4c, then run through
.BR iconv (3)
into the target encoding chosen by
.BR \-\-charset
(default
.IR ISO-8859-1 ).
The \fB//TRANSLIT\fR suffix is appended when the platform's iconv
supports it, so e.g. U+2014 EM DASH becomes a hyphen and U+00E9 stays
as 0xE9 in Latin\(hy1.  Un-mappable codepoints become `?'.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print a Markdown document with the bundled defaults:
.PP
.RS
.nf
escpmd README.md | lp -d pp404
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use the same printer state established by \fBescp\fR earlier in the
pipeline:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
    escp --init --left-margin 6 --cpi 12
    escpmd --no-init --no-ff doc.md
    escp --ff
} > /dev/usb/lp0
.fi
.RE
.PP
Print a small notebook of several files, one per page:
.PP
.RS
.nf
escpmd chapter*.md > book.bin
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 2 on any error (bad option, missing file, write error,
markdown parser failure).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR escp (1),
.IR docs/escpmd-mapping.md ,
.UR https://github.com/mity/md4c
md4c Markdown parser
.UE .
